  <section>
    <h2>How I built it</h2>
    <p>FastMCP + Claude Code. Each vulnerability gets one priority score from 0 to 100:</p>
    <pre><code>Priority score (0-100) = CVSS base score x 4 (up to 40 points)
                       + EPSS probability x 35 (up to 35 points)
                       + 25 points if the CVE is in CISA's KEV catalog</code></pre>
    <p>A KEV listing floors the score at 80, so known-exploited vulnerabilities are always critical. Buckets: critical (80+), high (60-79), medium (40-59), low (under 40), and unknown. Unknown means there isn't enough data to classify. It is not a low-risk verdict.</p>
    <p>Missing signals are named in the result and lower the confidence. They are never counted as zero. Only CVSS base metrics are used, because EPSS and KEV already cover exploit likelihood.</p>
    <p>Evaluation: a golden set of 17 hand-labeled entries (16 CVEs and 1 GHSA advisory) with expected priority buckets, plus an ordering check (Log4Shell above libwebp, both above lodash), using recorded fixtures (recorded 2026-10-21). Result: 17/17 matches, and the ordering check passes. This measures agreement with my own labels, not detection accuracy.</p>
  </section>

